{"name":"Zipline Green","hex":"#838567","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"zipline","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A pulley suspended on a cable mounted on an incline, designed to enable a user to travel from one point to another by means of gravity.","A trip on a zipline."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To move or travel by zipline."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/zipline"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Zip line","blocks":[{"text":"A zip-line, zip line, zip-wire, flying fox, or death slide is a pulley suspended on a cable, usually made of stainless steel, mounted on a slope. It is designed to enable cargo or a person propelled by gravity to travel from the top to the bottom of the inclined cable by holding on to, or being attached to, the freely moving pulley. It has been described as essentially a Tyrolean traverse that engages gravity to assist its speed of movement. Its use is not confined to adventure sport, recreation, or tourism, although modern-day usage tends to favor those meanings."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Texas_Zip_liner_5430.JPG/330px-Texas_Zip_liner_5430.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zip_line","wikidata":"Q1154882"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Zip-line_over_rainforest_canopy_4_January_2005%2C_Costa_Rica.jpg/500px-Zip-line_over_rainforest_canopy_4_January_2005%2C_Costa_Rica.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Zip-line_over_rainforest_canopy_4_January_2005%2C_Costa_Rica.jpg/500px-Zip-line_over_rainforest_canopy_4_January_2005%2C_Costa_Rica.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Zip-lining in Costa Rica, January 2005","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AZip-line_over_rainforest_canopy_4_January_2005%2C_Costa_Rica.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/Flying_foxes_across_river_chasm_in_Ladakh.jpg/500px-Flying_foxes_across_river_chasm_in_Ladakh.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/Flying_foxes_across_river_chasm_in_Ladak